title: Thread Pool
author: Charles Reiss
...

{.exercise ...}
Your task:

1.  Implement a "[thread pool](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thread_pool)" library with the following functions:

    *  `void pool_setup(int threads);`
        
        Create exactly `threads` threads, each of which will later run tasks submitted by `pool_submit_task`.

        You may assume that this is called before any of the below functions are called.

    *  `void pool_submit_task(const char *name, void *(*function)(void*), void *argument);

        Record a task as submitted so it will later be run by one of the threads created by the thread pool,
        then return.

        When the task is eventually run from one of the threads created by `pool_setup`,
        call the function specified by the function pointer `function` with the single argument `argument`.
        Then record the return value for later retreival with `pool_wait_for_task`.

        This function must make a copy of `name` (so it will not break if `name` is later modified
        or deallocated). You may assume the `name` is unique.

        This function must be safe to call from a task.

    *  `void *pool_wait_for_task(const char *name);`

        Wait for a task called `name` to complete running, if it hasn't alredy. You may assume the task
        was previously submitted using `pool_submit_task` and that this function is called
        exactly once for each task.

        Returns the value returned by the task's function when it was run.

        This function must be safe to call from a task.

    *  `void pool_stop();`

        Cause all threads in the thread pool to finish processing currently submitted tasks
        and then terminate. Wait for all the threads to be joined.

        Note that the tasks may still be waited for (to collect return values) after `pool_stop`
        returns.

        After `pool_stop()` returns and all tasks are waited for, it should be possible
        to call `pool_setup()` again (possibly with a different number of threads)
        and then submit and wait functions normally again.

        We do not care how this function behaves if new tasks are submitted after it is started
        but before a subsequent call to `pool_setup().`

    An example of how these functions might be used is given below.

    We also supply some code to maintain lists that you can use if you choose.

2.  Test your implementation. To help you with this, we supply [this test file](pool-files/pool-test.c). You could
    also write your own test.

3.  Submit your implementation using the submission site.

{/}

# Example of pool library usage

Suppose a user wanted to use the thread pool library to handle summing arrays of numbers. They might make
a function to do this like:

    struct SumInfo {
        int *array;
        int array_size;
    };

    void *sum_function(void *argument) {
        SumInfo *sum_info = argument;
        int sum = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < sum_info->array_size; i += 1) {
            sum += sum_info->array[i];
        }
        return (void*) sum;
    }

Then arrange for many sums to be computed by the thread pool:

    pool_setup(num_threads);

    struct SumInfo sum_A_info = { .array = arrayA, .array_size = ARRAY_SIZE };
    pool_submit_task("sum arrayA", sum_function, &sum_A_info);
    struct SumInfo sum_B_info = { .array = arrayB, .array_size = ARRAY_SIZE };
    pool_submit_task("sum arrayB", sum_function, &sum_B_info);
    ...

    int sum_A = (int) pool_wait_for_task("sum arrayA")
    int sum_B = (int) pool_wait_for_task("sum arrayB")
    ...

    pool_stop();

The thread pool will start exactly `num_threads` threads, regardless of how many arrays
there are to sum. Then it will have those threads run array-summing operations until
there are no operations left.

# Supplied list library

To aid your implementation, we supply some [linked list code](pool-files/tasklist.c) you can use in your implementation.

This code implements a doubly linked list of `struct Task`s, which have a `name` field. The linked list is represented by
a `struct TaskList`, which contains a head and tail pointer. We supply the following functions:

*  `list_is_empty`: return true if the list is empty
*  `list_remove`: remove the specified struct Task from the list. (Does not check the task is actually on the list.)
*  `list_remove_tail`: remove a node from the tail of the list and return it (or NULL if the list was empty)
*  `list_insert_head`: add an item at the head of the list
*  `list_find_named`: search the list for an item with the specified name value and return it (or NULL if no such item was found)
